GBTRACE canary diagnostics (schema v1)

Enable only custom_components.tuya_local.trace at DEBUG for a future canary.
The existing integration and TinyTuya debug loggers are outside this schema.
No deployment, restart, reload or logger configuration is performed by these tools.

Every message is GBTRACE followed by one JSON object. The exact envelope and
event-specific required fields are in gateway_trace_schema.json. Durations and
ts_ms use monotonic milliseconds. seq orders records within a process. run_id
is a random process token; all other IDs/slots are increasing counters. None
is allowed for gateway_slot/child_slot before acquisition or after unregister.
No identifier, name, address, DP ID/value, payload, key or exception text is
allowed in the trace. The emitter filters fields and catches sink failures.

Logical commands keep cmd_id through retries and across the worker boundary.
Request retries without cmd_id belong to polling/setup and are not counted as
logical outbound command retries by the analyzer.
call_id identifies an individual broker operation, lock_id one acquisition,
poll_id one safety poll, batch_id one nonempty drain, and push_id one received
message. pending_generation identifies one pending insertion batch; multiple
DPs in that batch share a generation. Replacements always get a fresh one.
pending_cmd_id records the original pending owner if an older cancelled
request's unsent state is included in a later command's snapshot.

mark_sent observes the unchanged production acceptance predicate. A rejected
old snapshot emits stale_mark_sent_ignored under its original cmd_id/generation.
push_ack is emitted only for matching gateway pushes, not status-poll data.
remaining_pending_count counts the still-observed pending properties belonging
to that logical command, not every child or every command on the gateway.
ack_latency_ms starts when the successful TinyTuya call returned. FAKE_IT_TIMEOUT
is unchanged: pending_expire/ack_timeout occur when normal pending-state access
actually removes expired sent state, not on a new background timer.

Health snapshots piggyback on broker activity at most once per 60 seconds,
plus a final snapshot after joining the worker. If the worker is blocked inside
TinyTuya, it cannot schedule another snapshot until it returns. Snapshot fields
are observations, not an atomic transaction across both threads. The analyzer
reports the final OBSERVED queue/pending counts, or null if not observed.

Usage (standard library only; keep the JSON schema beside the analyzer):
  python tools/analyze_gateway_trace.py saved-ha.log
  python tools/analyze_gateway_trace.py - < saved-ha.log

Output is deterministic JSON. Exit codes are PASS=0, WARN=1, FAIL=2. Only
GBTRACE JSON is parsed; malformed/foreign lines and fields are not echoed.
Latency distributions are milliseconds with linearly interpolated percentiles.
Polling's short window is one second, separately per process and gateway.
Commands.acknowledged counts commands whose observed remaining count reached
zero; push.acknowledged counts individual matched updates. Commands.ack_timeouts
counts logical commands with at least one expired update.

The analyzer detects missing command stages, unreleased locks, unresolved
broker operations, missing send results, sends without intervening retries,
unfinished batches/polls, bad lifecycle ordering, worker failure, and a rising
nondecreasing backlog in the last three health observations. Missing ACK alone,
ACK timeout, error completion or cancellation are WARN, never proof of failure.
Live gateways need not stop before a capture ends; started commands, calls,
lock acquisitions, polls and drain batches must finish for a clean result.

Limits: capture from before the relevant lifecycles through their completion.
Truncation, runtime logging changes or dropped log records can resemble missing
events. A sink failure is deliberately swallowed to preserve device behavior.
Logging consumes CPU and the configured log sink may add I/O latency. The
analyzer cannot certify a physical action, correlate a matching unsolicited
state change with causality, see TinyTuya-internal packet retransmissions, or
promise hardware latency. A 'send' is a TinyTuya control invocation; reconnect
events observe the broker's connection path, not every internal TinyTuya action.

The virtual tests model opaque blocking status calls, unlike TinyTuya's richer
wrong-CID buffering. Their starvation bound is one select wait plus all remaining
serialized status work in the finite burst, not a hardware latency SLA.
